{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21555","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21555","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21555","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco A. A.","familyName":"de Paula","name":"de Paula, Marco A. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mustapha","familyName":"Azreg-Aïnou","name":"Azreg-Aïnou, Mustapha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gravitational and kinematic redshifts of black holes in nonlinear electrodynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:13:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:57:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-09T07:02:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-10T00:29:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-25T07:48:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-28T00:33:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/1361-6382/aea467","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spacetimes arising from nonlinear electrodynamics (NED) are a good laboratory for studying both the nature of regular black hole (RBH) solutions and the imprints of nonlinear electromagnetic fields within this context. Over the past few decades, NED-sourced black hole (BH) spacetimes have attracted considerable attention, but electrically charged RBHs obtained in the so-called $F$ framework have been less addressed in the literature. We consider two electrically charged, fully regular members of the h-family that are solutions to general relativity minimally coupled to NED. Because of their potential astrophysical and astronomical applications, we mainly focus our investigation on the motion of photons and its implications for the gravitational and kinematic redshifts, as well as for the shadow radius, considering the effective geometry followed by photons in NED. Moreover, we consider the observational data for Sagittarius A$^{\\star}$ and Messier 87$^{\\star}$ BHs to impose some constraints on the charge-to-mass ratio of the fully RBHs. We obtain the general equations that govern the gravitational and kinematic redshifts of NED-sourced BH spacetimes. In particular, for fully RBHs with BH charge-to-mass ratios above some moderate value, we observe discrepancies in their physical and geometrical properties compared to those of the Reissner--Nordström (RN) BH. Therefore, our results indicate that it is possible to distinguish NED-based RBH solutions from RN BHs, from the perspective of gravitational and kinematic redshifts, suggesting potential astrophysical relevance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21555","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:23Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-28T02:32:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21536","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21536","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21536","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Narendhiran","familyName":"Vijayakumar","name":"Vijayakumar, Narendhiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AURASeg: Attention-Guided Upsampling with Residual-Assisted Boundary Refinement for Drivable-Area Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:01:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:56:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-09T06:18:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-12T01:18:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-14T01:03:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:10:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-06T02:32:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:17:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-25T11:26:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-08-26T00:51:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-08-31T06:23:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:39:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Free-space segmentation is essential for autonomous robots to identify drivable regions and navigate safely across indoor, outdoor, and road-scene environments. However, conventional encoder-decoder models often recover coarse region masks while losing the fine spatial information needed to localize drivable-area boundaries accurately. We propose Attention-Guided Upsampling with Residual-Assisted Boundary Refinement (AURASeg), a segmentation framework designed to preserve region-level accuracy while improving boundary quality. Built on a ResNet-18 encoder, AURASeg introduces an Attention Progressive Upsampling Decoder (APUD) that progressively combines semantic context with high-resolution spatial detail, together with a Residual Boundary Refinement Module (RBRM) that explicitly refines contour-sensitive features before final prediction. We evaluate AURASeg across indoor simulation, ground-robot imagery, and road-driving benchmarks. The results show that our proposed model remains competitive with established segmentation models on region-level metrics while providing particularly strong boundary localization, including in comparison with boundary-focused methods. Detailed ablations further demonstrate the role of the proposed decoding and refinement modules."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 4 figures, 6 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21536","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:57Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:01:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21231","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21231","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21231","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Hartline","name":"Hartline, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aleck","familyName":"Johnsen","name":"Johnsen, Aleck","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingkai","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yingkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scale-robust Auctions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Theoretical Economics (econ.TH)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:07:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:32:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-28T06:47:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T00:30:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study auctions that are robust at any scale, i.e., they can be applied to sell both expensive and cheap items and achieve the best multiplicative approximation of the optimal revenue in the worst case. We first show that it is without loss of optimality to restrict attention to scale-invariant mechanisms whenever the family of possible distributions is closed under every positive rescaling. This conclusion uses no regularity or other distributional shape restriction. We then solve the two-agent, single-item problem with values drawn i.i.d. from an unknown regular distribution when only a high value bidder can receive a positive allocation. The robustly optimal mechanism in this class randomizes between the second-price auction, with probability approximately 0.806, and a markup auction that offers the item to the highest-valued bidder at a price equal to 2.447 times the second-highest value. Its worst-case approximation ratio is approximately 1.907."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Part of the results from this work has appeared in FOCS'20 under the title \"Benchmark Design and Prior-independent Optimization\""}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21231","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:51Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-31T02:20:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21615","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21615","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21615","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Orest","familyName":"Kupyn","name":"Kupyn, Orest","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Théo","familyName":"Uscidda","name":"Uscidda, Théo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marta Tintore","familyName":"Gazulla","name":"Gazulla, Marta Tintore","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Manhardt","name":"Manhardt, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federico","familyName":"Tombari","name":"Tombari, Federico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Rupprecht","name":"Rupprecht, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Epipolar Geometry Improves Video Generation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:21:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:00:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-17T16:22:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T01:06:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video generation models have advanced significantly through the latent diffusion transformers trained with rectified flow techniques. Yet these models still struggle with geometric inconsistencies, unstable motion, and visual artifacts that break the illusion of realistic 3D scenes. 3D-consistent video generation could significantly impact numerous downstream applications in generation and reconstruction tasks. We explore how epipolar geometry constraints improve modern video diffusion models. Despite using massive training data, these models fail to capture fundamental geometric principles. We align diffusion models using pairwise epipolar geometry constraints via preference-based optimization, directly addressing unstable trajectories and geometric artifacts through mathematically principled geometric enforcement. Our approach efficiently enforces geometric principles without requiring end-to-end differentiability. Evaluation demonstrates that classical geometric constraints provide more stable optimization signals than modern learned metrics. Training on static scenes with dynamic cameras ensures metric quality while the model generalizes to various dynamic scenes. By bridging data-driven learning with classical computer vision, we reduce epipolar error by 31% and improve human-rated consistency from 54% to 72% without compromising visual quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21615","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:45Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:19:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21459","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21459","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21459","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adetayo","familyName":"Adebimpe","name":"Adebimpe, Adetayo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Helmut","familyName":"Neukirchen","name":"Neukirchen, Helmut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Welsh","name":"Welsh, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SBASH: a Framework for Designing and Evaluating RAG vs. Prompt-Tuned LLM Honeypots"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"K.6.5; D.4.6; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:41:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-04T00:45:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/fllm67465.2025.11391242","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Honeypots are decoy systems used for gathering valuable threat intelligence or diverting attackers away from production systems. Maximising attacker engagement is essential to their utility. However research has highlighted that context-awareness, such as the ability to respond to new attack types, systems and attacker agents, is necessary to increase engagement. Large Language Models (LLMs) have been shown as one approach to increase context awareness but suffer from several challenges including accuracy and timeliness of response time, high operational costs and data-protection issues due to cloud deployment. We propose the System-Based Attention Shell Honeypot (SBASH) framework which manages data-protection issues through the use of lightweight local LLMs. We investigate the use of Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) supported LLMs and non-RAG LLMs for Linux shell commands and evaluate them using several different metrics such as response time differences, realism from human testers, and similarity to a real system calculated with Levenshtein distance, SBert, and BertScore. We show that RAG improves accuracy for untuned models while models that have been tuned via a system prompt that tells the LLM to respond like a Linux system achieve without RAG a similar accuracy as untuned with RAG, while having a slightly lower latency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"to be published in: The 3rd International Conference on Foundation and Large Language Models (FLLM2025), IEEE, 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21459","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:55:13Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-04T03:29:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21372","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21372","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21372","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raphael","familyName":"Schmitt","name":"Schmitt, Raphael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HalleluBERT: Let Every Token That Has Meaning Bear Its Weight"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:52:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:44:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-01T11:52:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T02:02:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.63317/3qdqexx4e9i2","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer-based models have advanced NLP, yet Hebrew still lacks a RoBERTa encoder that is trained at scale and released in both base and large variants. We present HalleluBERT, a RoBERTa-based encoder family trained from scratch on 49.1~GB of deduplicated Hebrew web text and Wikipedia using a Hebrew-specific byte-level BPE vocabulary. On native Hebrew benchmarks for named entity recognition (BMC, NEMO) and sentiment classification (SMCD), HalleluBERT outperforms monolingual and multilingual baselines, and yields the highest unweighted mean score across the three benchmarks. We release model weights and tokenizer under the MIT license to support reproducible Hebrew NLP research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21372","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:53:10Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:53:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:54:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21364","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21364","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21364","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raphael","familyName":"Schmitt","name":"Schmitt, Raphael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Schweter","name":"Schweter, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SindBERT, the Sailor: Charting the Seas of Turkish NLP"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:48:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:44:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-01T11:38:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T02:01:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2026.sigturk-1.1","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer models have revolutionized NLP, yet many morphologically rich languages remain underrepresented in large-scale pre-training efforts. With SindBERT, we set out to chart the seas of Turkish NLP, providing the first large-scale RoBERTa-based encoder for Turkish. Trained from scratch on 312~GB of Turkish text (mC4, OSCAR23, Wikipedia), SindBERT is released in both base and large configurations, representing the first large-scale encoder-only language model available for Turkish. We evaluate SindBERT on part-of-speech tagging, named entity recognition, offensive language detection, and the TurBLiMP linguistic acceptability benchmark. Our results show that SindBERT performs competitively with existing Turkish and multilingual models, with the large variant achieving the best scores in two of four tasks but showing no consistent scaling advantage overall. This flat scaling trend, also observed for XLM-R and EuroBERT, suggests that current Turkish benchmarks may already be saturated. At the same time, comparisons with smaller but more curated models such as BERTurk highlight that corpus quality and diversity can outweigh sheer data volume. Taken together, SindBERT contributes both as an openly released resource for Turkish NLP and as an empirical case study on the limits of scaling and the central role of corpus composition in morphologically rich languages. The SindBERT models are released under the MIT license and made available in both fairseq and Huggingface formats."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at SIGTURK 2026, co-located with EACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21364","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:53:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:54:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21270","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21270","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21270","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinghao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenkun","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Chenkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaojun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Shaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoxiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhaoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiguo","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Shiguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangxu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Fangxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xipeng","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Xipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sparser Block-Sparse Attention via Token Permutation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T09:11:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:36:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-22T07:10:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-25T00:28:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scaling the context length of large language models (LLMs) offers significant benefits but is computationally expensive. This expense stems primarily from the self-attention mechanism, whose $O(N^2)$ complexity with respect to sequence length presents a major bottleneck for both memory and latency. Fortunately, the attention matrix is often sparse, particularly for long sequences, suggesting an opportunity for optimization. Block-sparse attention has emerged as a promising solution that partitions sequences into blocks and skips computation for a subset of these blocks. However, the effectiveness of this method is highly dependent on the underlying attention patterns, which can lead to sub-optimal block-level sparsity. For instance, important key tokens for queries within a single block may be scattered across numerous other blocks, leading to computational redundancy. In this work, we propose Permuted Block-Sparse Attention (\\textbf{PBS-Attn}), a plug-and-play method that leverages the permutation properties of attention to increase block-level sparsity and enhance the computational efficiency of LLM prefilling. We conduct comprehensive experiments on challenging real-world long-context datasets, demonstrating that PBS-Attn consistently outperforms existing block-sparse attention methods in model accuracy and closely matches the full attention baseline. Powered by our custom permuted-FlashAttention kernels, PBS-Attn achieves an end-to-end speedup of up to $2.75\\times$ in long-context prefilling, confirming its practical viability. Code available at https://github.com/xinghaow99/pbs-attn"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21270","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:50:46Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:50:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-25T02:09:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20933","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20933","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20933","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moin","familyName":"Safdar","name":"Safdar, Moin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shahzaib","familyName":"Iqbal","name":"Iqbal, Shahzaib","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mubeen","familyName":"Ghafoor","name":"Ghafoor, Mubeen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tariq M.","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Tariq M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Imran","familyName":"Razzak","name":"Razzak, Imran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thantrira","familyName":"Porntaveetus","name":"Porntaveetus, Thantrira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Alinejad-Rokny","name":"Alinejad-Rokny, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Focal Modulation and Bidirectional Feature Fusion Network for Medical Image Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T18:52:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:03:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-30T07:58:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-01T00:33:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical image segmentation is essential for clinical applications such as disease diagnosis, treatment planning, and disease development monitoring because it provides precise morphological and spatial information on anatomical structures that directly influence treatment decisions. Convolutional neural networks significantly impact image segmentation; however, since convolution operations are local, capturing global contextual information and long-range dependencies is still challenging. Their capacity to precisely segment structures with complicated borders and a variety of sizes is impacted by this restriction. Since transformers use self-attention methods to capture global context and long-range dependencies efficiently, integrating transformer-based architecture with CNNs is a feasible approach to overcoming these challenges. To address these challenges, we propose the Focal Modulation and Bidirectional Feature Fusion Network for Medical Image Segmentation, referred to as FM-BFF-Net in the remainder of this paper. The network combines convolutional and transformer components, employs a focal modulation attention mechanism to refine context awareness, and introduces a bidirectional feature fusion module that enables efficient interaction between encoder and decoder representations across scales. Through this design, FM-BFF-Net enhances boundary precision and robustness to variations in lesion size, shape, and contrast. Extensive experiments on eight publicly available datasets, including polyp detection, skin lesion segmentation, and ultrasound imaging, show that FM-BFF-Net consistently surpasses recent state-of-the-art methods in Jaccard index and Dice coefficient, confirming its effectiveness and adaptability for diverse medical imaging scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20933","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:42:51Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:42:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-01T02:32:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21356","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21356","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21356","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anupam","familyName":"Pani","name":"Pani, Anupam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanchao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yanchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gaze-VLM:Bridging Gaze and VLMs through Attention Regularization for Egocentric Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:33:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:43:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-24T13:37:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-25T01:00:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Eye gaze offers valuable cues about attention, short-term intent, and future actions, making it a powerful signal for modeling egocentric behavior. In this work, we propose a gaze-regularized framework that enhances VLMs for two key egocentric understanding tasks: fine-grained future event prediction and current activity understanding. Unlike prior approaches that rely solely on visual inputs or use gaze as an auxiliary input signal , our method uses gaze only during training. We introduce a gaze-regularized attention mechanism that aligns model focus with human visual gaze. This design is flexible and modular, allowing it to generalize across multiple VLM architectures that utilize attention. Experimental results show that our approach improves semantic prediction scores by up to 11 for future event prediction and around 7 for current activity understanding, compared to the corresponding baseline models trained without gaze regularization. These results highlight the value of gaze-guided training in improving the accuracy and robustness of egocentric VLMs. Overall, this work establishes a foundation for using human gaze to enhance the predictive capabilities of VLMs in real-world scenarios like assistive robots and human-machine collaboration. Code and additional information is available at: https://github.com/anupampani/Gaze-VLM"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21356","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:47Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-25T02:32:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanhao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Wanhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuteng","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Shuteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sen","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"More Than Memory Savings: Zeroth-Order Optimization Mitigates Forgetting in Continual Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T21:54:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:09:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-11T22:27:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:10:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-12T02:15:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-13T00:18:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Zeroth-order (ZO) optimization has gained attention as a memory-efficient alternative to first-order (FO) methods, particularly in settings where gradient computation is expensive or even impractical. Beyond its memory efficiency, in this work, we investigate ZO optimization for continual learning (CL) as a novel approach to address the plasticity-stability-efficiency trilemma. Through theoretical analysis and empirical evidence, we show that ZO optimization naturally leads to flatter loss landscapes, which in turn reduce forgetting in CL. However, this stability comes at a cost of plasticity: due to its imprecise gradient estimates and slower convergence, ZO optimization tends to be less effective than FO in acquiring new task-specific knowledge, particularly under constrained training budgets. To better understand this trade-off, we conduct a holistic evaluation of ZO optimization applied to various existing CL methods. Our findings reveal that ZO optimization enhances stability but often undermines plasticity, particularly when used with learnable classifiers. Motivated by this insight, we propose ZO-FC, a simple but effective approach that applies ZO optimization to a single adapter-based PEFT module with FO optimized classifier. This design leverages the stability benefits of ZO while preserving the adaptability of FO updates with negligible memory overhead. Experiments demonstrate that ZO-FC achieves an effective balance between stability and plasticity, offering a practical and memory-efficient solution for on-device CL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:44:51Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:44:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-13T02:20:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21303","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21303","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21303","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prakhar","familyName":"Ganesh","name":"Ganesh, Prakhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsiang","familyName":"Hsu","name":"Hsu, Hsiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Golnoosh","familyName":"Farnadi","name":"Farnadi, Golnoosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data as a Lever: A Neighbouring Datasets Perspective on Predictive Multiplicity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:01:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:39:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-02T08:22:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T02:42:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multiplicity, the existence of equally good yet competing models, has received growing attention in recent years. While prior work has emphasized modelling choices, the critical role of data in shaping multiplicity has been largely overlooked. In this work, we first introduce a neighbouring datasets framework, arguing that much of data processing can be reframed as choosing between neighbouring datasets. Under this framework, we find a counterintuitive theoretical relationship: neighbouring datasets with greater inter-class distribution overlap exhibit lower multiplicity.\n Building on this insight, we apply our framework to two domains: active learning and data imputation. For each, we establish natural extensions of the neighbouring datasets perspective, conduct the first systematic study of multiplicity in existing algorithms, and finally, propose novel multiplicity-aware methods, namely, multiplicity-aware data acquisition strategies for active learning and multiplicity-aware data imputation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21303","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:33Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:42:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21518","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21518","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21518","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Basile","name":"Basile, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentino","familyName":"Maiorca","name":"Maiorca, Valentino","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diego","familyName":"Doimo","name":"Doimo, Diego","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Locatello","name":"Locatello, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto","familyName":"Cazzaniga","name":"Cazzaniga, Alberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Head Pursuit: Probing Attention Specialization in Multimodal Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T14:41:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:54:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-14T15:47:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:48:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language and vision-language models have shown impressive performance across a wide range of tasks, but their internal mechanisms remain only partly understood. In this work, we study how individual attention heads in text-generative models specialize in specific semantic or visual attributes. Building on an established interpretability method, we reinterpret the practice of probing intermediate activations with the final decoding layer through the lens of signal processing. This lets us analyze multiple samples in a principled way and rank attention heads based on their relevance to target concepts. Our results show consistent patterns of specialization at the head level across both unimodal and multimodal transformers. Remarkably, we find that editing as few as 1% of the heads, selected using our method, can reliably suppress or enhance targeted concepts in the model output. We validate our approach on language tasks such as question answering and toxicity mitigation, as well as vision-language tasks including image classification and captioning. Our findings highlight an interpretable and controllable structure within attention layers, offering simple tools for understanding and editing large-scale generative models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 (spotlight)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21518","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:33Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:11:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21525","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21525","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21525","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huatian","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Huatian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiuh-Biing","familyName":"Sheu","name":"Sheu, Jiuh-Biing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoguang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xiaoguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Unified Model for Multi-Task Drone Routing in Post-Disaster Road Assessment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T14:48:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:55:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-12T04:16:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:21:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Post-disaster road assessment (PDRA) is essential for emergency response, enabling rapid evaluation of infrastructure conditions and efficient allocation of resources. Although drones provide a flexible and effective tool for PDRA, routing them in large-scale networks remains challenging. Exact and heuristic optimization methods scale poorly and demand domain expertise, while existing deep reinforcement learning (DRL) approaches adopt a single-task paradigm, requiring separate models for each problem variant and lacking adaptability to evolving operational needs. This study proposes a unified model (UM) for drone routing that simultaneously addresses eight PDRA variants. By training a single neural network across multiple problem configurations, UM captures shared structural knowledge while adapting to variant-specific constraints through a modern transformer encoder-decoder architecture. A lightweight adapter mechanism further enables efficient finetuning to unseen attributes without retraining, enhancing deployment flexibility in dynamic disaster scenarios. Extensive experiments demonstrate that the UM reduces training time and parameters by a factor of eight compared with training separate models, while consistently outperforming single-task DRL methods by 6-14%, heuristic algorithms by 22-42%, and commercial solvers by 24-82% in terms of solution quality (total collected information value). The model achieves rapid solutions (1-10 seconds) across networks of up to 1,000 nodes, with robustness confirmed through sensitivity analyses. Moreover, finetuning experiments show that unseen attributes can be effectively incorporated with minimal cost while retaining high solution quality. The source code for UM is publicly available at https://github.com/PJ-HTU/UM_PDRA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages, 8 figures,9 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21525","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:42Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-15T02:58:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21450","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21450","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21450","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federico","familyName":"Danieli","name":"Danieli, Federico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pau","familyName":"Rodriguez","name":"Rodriguez, Pau","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel","familyName":"Sarabia","name":"Sarabia, Miguel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xavier","familyName":"Suau","name":"Suau, Xavier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Zappella","name":"Zappella, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ParaRNN: Unlocking Parallel Training of Nonlinear RNNs for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 68W10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:28:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:50:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-03T09:47:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:26:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) laid the foundation for sequence modeling, but their intrinsic sequential nature restricts parallel computation, creating a fundamental barrier to scaling. This has led to the dominance of parallelizable architectures like Transformers and, more recently, State Space Models (SSMs). While SSMs achieve efficient parallelization through structured linear recurrences, this linearity constraint limits their expressive power and precludes modeling complex, nonlinear sequence-wise dependencies. To address this, we present ParaRNN, a framework that breaks the sequence-parallelization barrier for nonlinear RNNs. Building on prior work, we cast the sequence of nonlinear recurrence relationships as a single system of equations, which we solve in parallel using Newton's iterations combined with custom parallel reductions. Our implementation achieves speedups of up to 665x over naive sequential application, allowing training nonlinear RNNs at unprecedented scales. To showcase this, we apply ParaRNN to adaptations of LSTM and GRU architectures, successfully training models of 7B parameters that attain perplexity comparable to similarly-sized Transformers and Mamba2 architectures. To accelerate research in efficient sequence modeling, we release the ParaRNN codebase as an open-source framework for automatic training-parallelization of nonlinear RNNs, enabling researchers and practitioners to explore new nonlinear RNN models at scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21450","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:55:00Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-04T04:59:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21295","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21295","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21295","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stéphanie Laure Egome","familyName":"Nana","name":"Nana, Stéphanie Laure Egome","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnaud","familyName":"Leclerc","name":"Leclerc, Arnaud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo Ugo","familyName":"Ancarani","name":"Ancarani, Lorenzo Ugo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A complex Gaussian representation of continuum wavefunctions respectful of their asymptotic behaviour"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T09:44:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:38:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-03T11:40:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:31:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/bs.aiq.2025.03.009","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Complex Gaussian basis sets are optimized to accurately represent continuum radial wavefunctions over the whole space. First, attention is put on the technical ability of the optimization method to get more flexible series of Gaussian exponents, in order to improve the accuracy of the fitting approach. Second, an indirect fitting method is proposed, allowing for the oscillatory behaviour of continuum functions to be conserved up to infinity as a factorized asymptotic function, while the Gaussian representation is applied to some appropriately defined distortion factor with limited spatial extension. As an illustration, the method is applied to radial Coulomb functions with realistic energy parameters. We also show that the indirect fitting approach keeps the advantageous analytical structure of typical one-electron transition integrals occurring in molecular ionization applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21295","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:22Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-04T04:59:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21246","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21246","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21246","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Külper","name":"Külper, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan-Niclas","familyName":"Hilgert","name":"Hilgert, Jan-Niclas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frank","familyName":"Breitinger","name":"Breitinger, Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Lambertz","name":"Lambertz, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What's Next, Cloud? A Forensic Framework for Analyzing Self-Hosted Cloud Storage Solutions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:30:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:34:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T18:31:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:03:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-hosted cloud storage platforms like Nextcloud are gaining popularity among individuals and organizations seeking greater control over their data. However, this shift introduces new challenges for digital forensic investigations, particularly in systematically analyzing both client and server components. Despite Nextcloud's widespread use, it has received limited attention in forensic research. In this work, we critically examine existing cloud storage forensic frameworks and highlight their limitations. To address the gaps, we propose an extended forensic framework that incorporates device monitoring and leverages cloud APIs for structured, repeatable evidence acquisition. Using Nextcloud as a case study, we demonstrate how its native APIs can be used to reliably access forensic artifacts, and we introduce an open-source acquisition tool that implements this approach. Our framework equips investigators with a more flexible method for analyzing self-hosted cloud storage systems, and offers a foundation for further development in this evolving area of digital forensics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21246","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:50:13Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:50:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-31T02:11:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20958","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20958","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20958","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asif","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Asif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farhan","familyName":"Ishtiaque","name":"Ishtiaque, Farhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md. Muhyminul","familyName":"Haque","name":"Haque, Md. Muhyminul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farhana","familyName":"Sarker","name":"Sarker, Farhana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ravi","familyName":"Vaidyanathan","name":"Vaidyanathan, Ravi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khondaker A.","familyName":"Mamun","name":"Mamun, Khondaker A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NeuroPilot: A Realtime Brain-Computer Interface system to enhance concentration of students in online learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T19:36:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:05:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:13:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T04:59:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The prevalence of online learning poses a vital challenge in real-time monitoring of students' concentration. Traditional methods such as questionnaire assessments require manual intervention, and webcam-based monitoring fails to provide accurate insights about learners' mental focus as it is deceived by mere screen fixation without cognitive engagement. Existing BCI-based approaches lack real-time validation and evaluation procedures. To address these limitations, a Brain-Computer Interface (BCI) system is developed using a non-invasive Electroencephalogram (EEG) headband, FocusCalm, to record brainwave activity under attentive and non-attentive states. 20 minutes of data were collected from each of 20 participants watching a pre-recorded educational video. The data validation employed a novel intra-video questionnaire assessment. Subsequently, collected signals were segmented (sliding window), filtered (Butterworth bandpass), and cleaned (removal of high-amplitude and EOG artifacts such as eye blinks). Time, frequency, wavelet, and statistical features were extracted, followed by recursive feature elimination (RFE) with support vector machines (SVMs) to classify attention and non-attention states. The leave-one-subject-out (LOSO) cross-validation accuracy was found to be 88.77%. The system provides feedback alerts upon detection of a non-attention state and maintains focus profile logs. A pilot study was conducted to evaluate the effectiveness of real-time feedback. Five participants underwent a 10-minute session comprising a 5-minute baseline phase devoid of feedback, succeeded by a 5-minute feedback phase, during which alerts were activated if participants exhibited inattention for approximately 8 consecutive seconds. A paired t-test (t = 5.73, p = 0.007) indicated a statistically significant improvement in concentration during the feedback phase."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"V2: Author list updated with consent from all authors. Minor grammatical errors fixed. This work has been submitted to the IEEE for possible publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20958","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:26Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T02:19:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20931","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20931","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20931","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Albert","familyName":"Reuther","name":"Reuther, Albert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Michaleas","name":"Michaleas, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Jones","name":"Jones, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay","familyName":"Gadepally","name":"Gadepally, Vijay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeremy","familyName":"Kepner","name":"Kepner, Jeremy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lincoln AI Computing Survey (LAICS) and Trends"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"C.1.4; C.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T18:43:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:58:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/hpec67600.2025.11196427","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the past year, generative AI (GenAI) models have received a tremendous amount of attention, which in turn has increased attention to computing systems for training and inference for GenAI. Hence, an update to this survey is due. This paper is an update of the survey of AI accelerators and processors from past seven years, which is called the Lincoln AI Computing Survey -- LAICS (pronounced \"lace\"). This multi-year survey collects and summarizes the current commercial accelerators that have been publicly announced with peak performance and peak power consumption numbers. In the same tradition of past papers of this survey, the performance and power values are plotted on a scatter graph, and a number of dimensions and observations from the trends on this plot are again discussed and analyzed. Market segments are highlighted on the scatter plot, and zoomed plots of each segment are also included. A brief description of each of the new accelerators that have been added in the survey this year is included, and this update features a new categorization of computing architectures that implement each of the accelerators."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 7 figures, 2025 IEEE High Performance Extreme Computing (HPEC) conference, September 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20931","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:42:49Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:42:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T02:19:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21264","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21264","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21264","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyu","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Kaiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanjiang","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Hanjiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yaqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuangjian","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Chuangjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan Pan Kun","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Yan Pan Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Topology Sculptor, Shape Refiner: Discrete Diffusion Model for High-Fidelity 3D Meshes Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:51:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:36:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T16:38:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:54:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we introduce Topology Sculptor, Shape Refiner (TSSR), a novel method for generating high-quality, artist-style 3D meshes based on Discrete Diffusion Models (DDMs). Our primary motivation for TSSR is to achieve highly accurate token prediction while enabling parallel generation, a significant advantage over sequential autoregressive methods. By allowing TSSR to \"see\" all mesh tokens concurrently, we unlock a new level of efficiency and control. We leverage this parallel generation capability through three key innovations: 1) Decoupled Training and Hybrid Inference, which distinctly separates the DDM-based generation into a topology sculpting stage and a subsequent shape refinement stage. This strategic decoupling enables TSSR to effectively capture both intricate local topology and overarching global shape. 2) An Improved Hourglass Architecture, featuring bidirectional attention enriched by face-vertex-sequence level Rotational Positional Embeddings (RoPE), thereby capturing richer contextual information across the mesh structure. 3) A novel Connection Loss, which acts as a topological constraint to further enhance the realism and fidelity of the generated meshes. Extensive experiments on complex datasets demonstrate that TSSR generates high-quality 3D artist-style meshes, capable of achieving up to 10,000 faces at a remarkable spatial resolution of $1024^3$. The code will be released at: https://github.com/psky1111/Tencent-TSSR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21264","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:50:38Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:50:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T04:06:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21696","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21696","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21696","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xibin","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yifu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongdong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BachVid: Training-Free Video Generation with Consistent Background and Character"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:56:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:05:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion Transformers (DiTs) have recently driven significant progress in text-to-video (T2V) generation. However, generating multiple videos with consistent characters and backgrounds remains a significant challenge. Existing methods typically rely on reference images or extensive training, and often only address character consistency, leaving background consistency to image-to-video models. We introduce BachVid, the first training-free method that achieves consistent video generation without needing any reference images. Our approach is based on a systematic analysis of DiT's attention mechanism and intermediate features, revealing its ability to extract foreground masks and identify matching points during the denoising process. Our method leverages this finding by first generating an identity video and caching the intermediate variables, and then inject these cached variables into corresponding positions in newly generated videos, ensuring both foreground and background consistency across multiple videos. Experimental results demonstrate that BachVid achieves robust consistency in generated videos without requiring additional training, offering a novel and efficient solution for consistent video generation without relying on reference images or additional training."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://wolfball.github.io/bachvid"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21696","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T02:00:39Z","registered":"2025-10-27T02:00:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:00:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21693","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21693","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21693","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reuben","familyName":"Narad","name":"Narad, Reuben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonard","familyName":"Boussioux","name":"Boussioux, Leonard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural TSP Solvers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:54:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:05:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural networks have advanced combinatorial optimization, with Transformer-based solvers achieving near-optimal solutions on the Traveling Salesman Problem (TSP) in milliseconds. However, these models operate as black boxes, providing no insight into the geometric patterns they learn or the heuristics they employ during tour construction. We address this opacity by applying sparse autoencoders (SAEs), a mechanistic interpretability technique, to a Transformer-based TSP solver, representing the first application of activation-based interpretability methods to operations research models. We train a pointer network with reinforcement learning on 100-node instances, then fit an SAE to the encoder's residual stream to discover an overcomplete dictionary of interpretable features. Our analysis reveals that the solver naturally develops features mirroring fundamental TSP concepts: boundary detectors that activate on convex-hull nodes, cluster-sensitive features responding to locally dense regions, and separator features encoding geometric partitions. These findings provide the first model-internal account of what neural TSP solvers compute before node selection, demonstrate that geometric structure emerges without explicit supervision, and suggest pathways toward transparent hybrid systems that combine neural efficiency with algorithmic interpretability. Interactive feature explorer: https://reubennarad.github.io/TSP_interp"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21693","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T02:00:34Z","registered":"2025-10-27T02:00:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:00:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21654","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21654","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21654","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxi","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jiaxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rayan","familyName":"Armani","name":"Armani, Rayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dominik","familyName":"Hollidt","name":"Hollidt, Dominik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-Chi","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Yi-Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Holz","name":"Holz, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Group Inertial Poser: Multi-Person Pose and Global Translation from Sparse Inertial Sensors and Ultra-Wideband Ranging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2; I.3; I.4; I.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 68T45, 68U01","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:11:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:02:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tracking human full-body motion using sparse wearable inertial measurement units (IMUs) overcomes the limitations of occlusion and instrumentation of the environment inherent in vision-based approaches. However, purely IMU-based tracking compromises translation estimates and accurate relative positioning between individuals, as inertial cues are inherently self-referential and provide no direct spatial reference for others. In this paper, we present a novel approach for robustly estimating body poses and global translation for multiple individuals by leveraging the distances between sparse wearable sensors - both on each individual and across multiple individuals. Our method Group Inertial Poser estimates these absolute distances between pairs of sensors from ultra-wideband ranging (UWB) and fuses them with inertial observations as input into structured state-space models to integrate temporal motion patterns for precise 3D pose estimation. Our novel two-step optimization further leverages the estimated distances for accurately tracking people's global trajectories through the world. We also introduce GIP-DB, the first IMU+UWB dataset for two-person tracking, which comprises 200 minutes of motion recordings from 14 participants. In our evaluation, Group Inertial Poser outperforms previous state-of-the-art methods in accuracy and robustness across synthetic and real-world data, showing the promise of IMU+UWB-based multi-human motion capture in the wild. Code, models, dataset: https://github.com/eth-siplab/GroupInertialPoser"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICCV 2025, Code: https://github.com/eth-siplab/GroupInertialPoser"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21654","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:59:39Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:59:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:59:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21581","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21581","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21581","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ciara","familyName":"Rowles","name":"Rowles, Ciara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varun","familyName":"Jampani","name":"Jampani, Varun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Donné","name":"Donné, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shimon","familyName":"Vainer","name":"Vainer, Shimon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"Parker","name":"Parker, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zach","familyName":"Evans","name":"Evans, Zach","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Foley Control: Aligning a Frozen Latent Text-to-Audio Model to Video"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:49:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:58:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foley Control is a lightweight approach to video-guided Foley that keeps pretrained single-modality models frozen and learns only a small cross-attention bridge between them. We connect V-JEPA2 video embeddings to a frozen Stable Audio Open DiT text-to-audio (T2A) model by inserting compact video cross-attention after the model's existing text cross-attention, so prompts set global semantics while video refines timing and local dynamics. The frozen backbones retain strong marginals (video; audio given text) and the bridge learns the audio-video dependency needed for synchronization -- without retraining the audio prior. To cut memory and stabilize training, we pool video tokens before conditioning. On curated video-audio benchmarks, Foley Control delivers competitive temporal and semantic alignment with far fewer trainable parameters than recent multi-modal systems, while preserving prompt-driven controllability and production-friendly modularity (swap/upgrade encoders or the T2A backbone without end-to-end retraining). Although we focus on Video-to-Foley, the same bridge design can potentially extend to other audio modalities (e.g., speech)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://stability-ai.github.io/foleycontrol.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21581","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:58:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21544","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21544","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21544","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rushikesh","familyName":"Ubale","name":"Ubale, Rushikesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasar","familyName":"Mulani","name":"Mulani, Yasar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhay","familyName":"Suresh","name":"Suresh, Abhay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gregory","familyName":"Byrd","name":"Byrd, Gregory","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangram","familyName":"Deshpande","name":"Deshpande, Sangram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"B. R.","familyName":"Nikilesh","name":"Nikilesh, B. R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanya","familyName":"Nanda","name":"Nanda, Sanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum Similarity-Driven QUBO Framework for Multi-Period Supply Chain Allocation using Time-Multiplexed Coherent Ising Machines and Simulated Quantum Annealing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.2.2; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"90C20, 90C59","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:04:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:56:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-period stock-keeping unit (SKU) allocation in supply chains is a combinatorial optimization problem that is both NP-hard and operationally critical, requiring simultaneous attention to profitability, feasibility, and diversity. Quadratic unconstrained binary optimization (QUBO) provides a principled framework for such tasks, yet prior studies often rely on simplified assumptions or omit real operational constraints.\n This work proposes a hybrid QUBO framework integrating three advances: (i) a quantum-derived similarity kernel, obtained from a variational RX embedding, to discourage redundant SKU selections; (ii) exact per-period capacity enforcement via slack-bit encoding to maintain feasibility; and (iii) execution on a time-multiplexed Coherent Ising Machine (CIM) benchmarked against simulated quantum annealing (SQA) and classical optimization algorithms. The resulting model, with over one million quadratic terms and about 4,100 variables, captures profit, risk, and capacity interactions within a unified formulation.\n On a dataset of 500 SKUs across eight planning periods, Quanfluence's CIM achieved an energy of minus 2.95 times 10 to the power of 16, producing robust solutions with 288 distinct SKUs (approximately 60 percent of the catalog), 226,813 allocated units, and 12.75 million dollars profit, all with zero capacity violations. These results demonstrate that hybrid quantum-classical QUBO methods can deliver feasible and profitable supply-chain allocations at an industrial scale."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, 8 figures KeyWords: Quantum-inspired optimization, QUBO, Supply chain management, Coherent Ising Machine, Simulated Quantum Annealing, SKU allocation, Quantum similarity kernel, Slack-bit encoding"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21544","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:08Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21542","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21542","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21542","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johann Flemming","familyName":"Gloy","name":"Gloy, Johann Flemming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Olsson","name":"Olsson, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HollowFlow: Efficient Sample Likelihood Evaluation using Hollow Message Passing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:04:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:56:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Flow and diffusion-based models have emerged as powerful tools for scientific applications, particularly for sampling non-normalized probability distributions, as exemplified by Boltzmann Generators (BGs). A critical challenge in deploying these models is their reliance on sample likelihood computations, which scale prohibitively with system size $n$, often rendering them infeasible for large-scale problems. To address this, we introduce $\\textit{HollowFlow}$, a flow-based generative model leveraging a novel non-backtracking graph neural network (NoBGNN). By enforcing a block-diagonal Jacobian structure, HollowFlow likelihoods are evaluated with a constant number of backward passes in $n$, yielding speed-ups of up to $\\mathcal{O}(n^2)$: a significant step towards scaling BGs to larger systems. Crucially, our framework generalizes: $\\textbf{any equivariant GNN or attention-based architecture}$ can be adapted into a NoBGNN. We validate HollowFlow by training BGs on two different systems of increasing size. For both systems, the sampling and likelihood evaluation time decreases dramatically, following our theoretical scaling laws. For the larger system we obtain a $10^2\\times$ speed-up, clearly illustrating the potential of HollowFlow-based approaches for high-dimensional scientific problems previously hindered by computational bottlenecks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21542","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:05Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21538","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21538","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21538","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Likun","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Likun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuan-Wei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kuan-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joy","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Joy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InterpDetect: Interpretable Signals for Detecting Hallucinations in Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:02:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:56:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) integrates external knowledge to mitigate hallucinations, yet models often generate outputs inconsistent with retrieved content. Accurate hallucination detection requires disentangling the contributions of external context and parametric knowledge, which prior methods typically conflate. We investigate the mechanisms underlying RAG hallucinations and find they arise when later-layer FFN modules disproportionately inject parametric knowledge into the residual stream. To address this, we explore a mechanistic detection approach based on external context scores and parametric knowledge scores. Using Qwen3-0.6b, we compute these scores across layers and attention heads and train regression-based classifiers to predict hallucinations. Our method is evaluated against state-of-the-art LLMs (GPT-5, GPT-4.1) and detection baselines (RAGAS, TruLens, RefChecker). Furthermore, classifiers trained on Qwen3-0.6b signals generalize to GPT-4.1-mini responses, demonstrating the potential of proxy-model evaluation. Our results highlight mechanistic signals as efficient, generalizable predictors for hallucination detection in RAG systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21538","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21501","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21501","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21501","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanghao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Guanghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mingxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoyu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ruoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peisen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Peisen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenrui","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Wenrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junni","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Junni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongkai","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Hongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaopeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaopeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GranViT: A Fine-Grained Vision Model With Autoregressive Perception For MLLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T12:33:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:53:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision encoders are indispensable for allowing impressive performance of Multi-modal Large Language Models (MLLMs) in vision language tasks such as visual question answering and reasoning. However, existing vision encoders focus on global image representations but overlook fine-grained regional analysis. They are limited in fine grained perception due to the scarcity of fine grained annotated data and the lack of a fine grained pre-training paradigm. In this paper, we propose GranViT, a novel Vision Transformer that integrates fine-grained feature extraction with semantic alignment to Large Language Models (LLMs) via region level autoregressive training. We first construct Gran-29M, a dataset comprising 2million natural and OCR images paired with over 180 million high-quality region-level annotations, to enable large scale fine grained pretraining. Consequently, we develop a pretraining-adaptation framework along with a self distillation mechanism to train fine-grained GranViT on Gran-29M. We sufficiently exploit the fine-grained annotations from Gran-29M to resort to bounding-box-to-caption regression to enhance localized visual representation of the vision encoder in the pretraining and caption-to-bounding-box regression to improve vision feature utilization and localization for LLM in the adaptation. We further incorporate a self distillation mechanism that imposes explicit localization constraints on the vision encoder to strengthen its regional reasoning capability. Extensive experiments show that GranViT surpasses existing vision encoders and attains strong transferability to varying LLMs. Remarkably, it achieves state-of-the-art results on fine-grained recognition, multimodal VQA, and OCR understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21501","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:10Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:56:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21495","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21495","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21495","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jitai","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jitai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hua","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Hua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sida","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Sida","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiwen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kaiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Automatic Detection Method for Hematoma Features in Placental Abruption Ultrasound Images Based on Few-Shot Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T14:20:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:53:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Placental abruption is a severe complication during pregnancy, and its early accurate diagnosis is crucial for ensuring maternal and fetal safety. Traditional ultrasound diagnostic methods heavily rely on physician experience, leading to issues such as subjective bias and diagnostic inconsistencies. This paper proposes an improved model, EH-YOLOv11n (Enhanced Hemorrhage-YOLOv11n), based on small-sample learning, aiming to achieve automatic detection of hematoma features in placental ultrasound images. The model enhances performance through multidimensional optimization: it integrates wavelet convolution and coordinate convolution to strengthen frequency and spatial feature extraction; incorporates a cascaded group attention mechanism to suppress ultrasound artifacts and occlusion interference, thereby improving bounding box localization accuracy. Experimental results demonstrate a detection accuracy of 78%, representing a 2.5% improvement over YOLOv11n and a 13.7% increase over YOLOv8. The model exhibits significant superiority in precision-recall curves, confidence scores, and occlusion scenarios. Combining high accuracy with real-time processing, this model provides a reliable solution for computer-aided diagnosis of placental abruption, holding significant clinical application value."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21495","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:01Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:56:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21479","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21479","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21479","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yating","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yating","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qijun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Qijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lintao","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Lintao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hujun","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Hujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ITC-RWKV: Interactive Tissue-Cell Modeling with Recurrent Key-Value Aggregation for Histopathological Subtyping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T14:03:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:52:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate interpretation of histopathological images demands integration of information across spatial and semantic scales, from nuclear morphology and cellular textures to global tissue organization and disease-specific patterns. Although recent foundation models in pathology have shown strong capabilities in capturing global tissue context, their omission of cell-level feature modeling remains a key limitation for fine-grained tasks such as cancer subtype classification. To address this, we propose a dual-stream architecture that models the interplay between macroscale tissue features and aggregated cellular representations. To efficiently aggregate information from large cell sets, we propose a receptance-weighted key-value aggregation model, a recurrent transformer that captures inter-cell dependencies with linear complexity. Furthermore, we introduce a bidirectional tissue-cell interaction module to enable mutual attention between localized cellular cues and their surrounding tissue environment. Experiments on four histopathological subtype classification benchmarks show that the proposed method outperforms existing models, demonstrating the critical role of cell-level aggregation and tissue-cell interaction in fine-grained computational pathology."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accept by BMVC 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21479","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:55:40Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:55:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21453","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21453","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21453","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiguang","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Zhiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyang","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Chengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Liu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peilin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Peilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yize","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yize","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangzhen","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Fangzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Task Vehicle Routing Solver via Mixture of Specialized Experts under State-Decomposable MDP"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:31:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:50:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing neural methods for multi-task vehicle routing problems (VRPs) typically learn unified solvers to handle multiple constraints simultaneously. However, they often underutilize the compositional structure of VRP variants, each derivable from a common set of basis VRP variants. This critical oversight causes unified solvers to miss out the potential benefits of basis solvers, each specialized for a basis VRP variant. To overcome this limitation, we propose a framework that enables unified solvers to perceive the shared-component nature across VRP variants by proactively reusing basis solvers, while mitigating the exponential growth of trained neural solvers. Specifically, we introduce a State-Decomposable MDP (SDMDP) that reformulates VRPs by expressing the state space as the Cartesian product of basis state spaces associated with basis VRP variants. More crucially, this formulation inherently yields the optimal basis policy for each basis VRP variant. Furthermore, a Latent Space-based SDMDP extension is developed by incorporating both the optimal basis policies and a learnable mixture function to enable the policy reuse in the latent space. Under mild assumptions, this extension provably recovers the optimal unified policy of SDMDP through the mixture function that computes the state embedding as a mapping from the basis state embeddings generated by optimal basis policies. For practical implementation, we introduce the Mixture-of-Specialized-Experts Solver (MoSES), which realizes basis policies through specialized Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) experts, and implements the mixture function via an adaptive gating mechanism. Extensive experiments conducted across VRP variants showcase the superiority of MoSES over prior methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21453","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:55:05Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:55:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21449","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21449","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21449","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengtian","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shengtian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingshi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yingshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingrou","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingrou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoniTor: Exploiting Large Language Models with Instruction for Online Video Anomaly Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:28:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:50:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video Anomaly Detection (VAD) aims to locate unusual activities or behaviors within videos. Recently, offline VAD has garnered substantial research attention, which has been invigorated by the progress in large language models (LLMs) and vision-language models (VLMs), offering the potential for a more nuanced understanding of anomalies. However, online VAD has seldom received attention due to real-time constraints and computational intensity. In this paper, we introduce a novel Memory-based online scoring queue scheme for Training-free VAD (MoniTor), to address the inherent complexities in online VAD. Specifically, MoniTor applies a streaming input to VLMs, leveraging the capabilities of pre-trained large-scale models. To capture temporal dependencies more effectively, we incorporate a novel prediction mechanism inspired by Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks. This ensures the model can effectively model past states and leverage previous predictions to identify anomalous behaviors. Thereby, it better understands the current frame. Moreover, we design a scoring queue and an anomaly prior to dynamically store recent scores and cover all anomalies in the monitoring scenario, providing guidance for LLMs to distinguish between normal and abnormal behaviors over time. We evaluate MoniTor on two large datasets (i.e., UCF-Crime and XD-Violence) containing various surveillance and real-world scenarios. The results demonstrate that MoniTor outperforms state-of-the-art methods and is competitive with weakly supervised methods without training. Code is available at https://github.com/YsTvT/MoniTor."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025. The first two authors hold equal contributions"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21449","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:55:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21448","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21448","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21448","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuojing","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Zhuojing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yushu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unified token representations for sequential decision models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:25:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:50:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers have demonstrated strong potential in offline reinforcement learning (RL) by modeling trajectories as sequences of return-to-go, states, and actions. However, existing approaches such as the Decision Transformer(DT) and its variants suffer from redundant tokenization and quadratic attention complexity, limiting their scalability in real-time or resource-constrained settings. To address this, we propose a Unified Token Representation (UTR) that merges return-to-go, state, and action into a single token, substantially reducing sequence length and model complexity. Theoretical analysis shows that UTR leads to a tighter Rademacher complexity bound, suggesting improved generalization. We further develop two variants: UDT and UDC, built upon transformer and gated CNN backbones, respectively. Both achieve comparable or superior performance to state-of-the-art methods with markedly lower computation. These findings demonstrate that UTR generalizes well across architectures and may provide an efficient foundation for scalable control in future large decision models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21448","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:58Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21440","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21440","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21440","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"Trappolini","name":"Trappolini, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florin","familyName":"Cuconasu","name":"Cuconasu, Florin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Filice","name":"Filice, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoelle","familyName":"Maarek","name":"Maarek, Yoelle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabrizio","familyName":"Silvestri","name":"Silvestri, Fabrizio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Redefining Retrieval Evaluation in the Era of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:17:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:49:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Traditional Information Retrieval (IR) metrics, such as nDCG, MAP, and MRR, assume that human users sequentially examine documents with diminishing attention to lower ranks. This assumption breaks down in Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) systems, where search results are consumed by Large Language Models (LLMs), which, unlike humans, process all retrieved documents as a whole rather than sequentially. Additionally, traditional IR metrics do not account for related but irrelevant documents that actively degrade generation quality, rather than merely being ignored. Due to these two major misalignments, namely human vs. machine position discount and human relevance vs. machine utility, classical IR metrics do not accurately predict RAG performance. We introduce a utility-based annotation schema that quantifies both the positive contribution of relevant passages and the negative impact of distracting ones. Building on this foundation, we propose UDCG (Utility and Distraction-aware Cumulative Gain), a metric using an LLM-oriented positional discount to directly optimize the correlation with the end-to-end answer accuracy. Experiments on five datasets and six LLMs demonstrate that UDCG improves correlation by up to 36% compared to traditional metrics. Our work provides a critical step toward aligning IR evaluation with LLM consumers and enables more reliable assessment of RAG components"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21440","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:47Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21403","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21403","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21403","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jieyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jieyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanwen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hanwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Malu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Malu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haizhou","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unveiling the Spatial-temporal Effective Receptive Fields of Spiking Neural Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T12:46:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:47:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs) demonstrate significant potential for energy-efficient neuromorphic computing through an event-driven paradigm. While training methods and computational models have greatly advanced, SNNs struggle to achieve competitive performance in visual long-sequence modeling tasks. In artificial neural networks, the effective receptive field (ERF) serves as a valuable tool for analyzing feature extraction capabilities in visual long-sequence modeling. Inspired by this, we introduce the Spatio-Temporal Effective Receptive Field (ST-ERF) to analyze the ERF distributions across various Transformer-based SNNs. Based on the proposed ST-ERF, we reveal that these models suffer from establishing a robust global ST-ERF, thereby limiting their visual feature modeling capabilities. To overcome this issue, we propose two novel channel-mixer architectures: \\underline{m}ulti-\\underline{l}ayer-\\underline{p}erceptron-based m\\underline{ixer} (MLPixer) and \\underline{s}plash-and-\\underline{r}econstruct \\underline{b}lock (SRB). These architectures enhance global spatial ERF through all timesteps in early network stages of Transformer-based SNNs, improving performance on challenging visual long-sequence modeling tasks. Extensive experiments conducted on the Meta-SDT variants and across object detection and semantic segmentation tasks further validate the effectiveness of our proposed method. Beyond these specific applications, we believe the proposed ST-ERF framework can provide valuable insights for designing and optimizing SNN architectures across a broader range of tasks. The code is available at \\href{https://github.com/EricZhang1412/Spatial-temporal-ERF}{\\faGithub~EricZhang1412/Spatial-temporal-ERF}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Acceped by 39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21403","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:53:54Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:53:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:53:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21388","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21388","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21388","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arshdeep","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Arshdeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinayak","familyName":"Abrol","name":"Abrol, Vinayak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark D.","familyName":"Plumbley","name":"Plumbley, Mark D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compressing Quaternion Convolutional Neural Networks for Audio Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T12:19:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:46:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conventional Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) in the real domain have been widely used for audio classification. However, their convolution operations process multi-channel inputs independently, limiting the ability to capture correlations among channels. This can lead to suboptimal feature learning, particularly for complex audio patterns such as multi-channel spectrogram representations. Quaternion Convolutional Neural Networks (QCNNs) address this limitation by employing quaternion algebra to jointly capture inter-channel dependencies, enabling more compact models with fewer learnable parameters while better exploiting the multi-dimensional nature of audio signals. However, QCNNs exhibit higher computational complexity due to the overhead of quaternion operations, resulting in increased inference latency and reduced efficiency compared to conventional CNNs, posing challenges for deployment on resource-constrained platforms. To address this challenge, this study explores knowledge distillation (KD) and pruning, to reduce the computational complexity of QCNNs while maintaining performance. Our experiments on audio classification reveal that pruning QCNNs achieves similar or superior performance compared to KD while requiring less computational effort. Compared to conventional CNNs and Transformer-based architectures, pruned QCNNs achieve competitive performance with a reduced learnable parameter count and computational complexity. On the AudioSet dataset, pruned QCNNs reduce computational cost by 50\\% and parameter count by 80\\%, while maintaining performance comparable to the conventional CNNs. Furthermore, pruned QCNNs generalize well across multiple audio classification benchmarks, including GTZAN for music genre recognition, ESC-50 for environmental sound classification and RAVDESS for speech emotion recognition."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under review in IEEE TASLPRO"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21388","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:53:33Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:53:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:53:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21351","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21351","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21351","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongxin","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Tongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingyi","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Lingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pinxue","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Pinxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haijing","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Haijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixun","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Kaixun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wenqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamic Semantic-Aware Correlation Modeling for UAV Tracking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:28:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:43:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"UAV tracking can be widely applied in scenarios such as disaster rescue, environmental monitoring, and logistics transportation. However, existing UAV tracking methods predominantly emphasize speed and lack exploration in semantic awareness, which hinders the search region from extracting accurate localization information from the template. The limitation results in suboptimal performance under typical UAV tracking challenges such as camera motion, fast motion, and low resolution, etc. To address this issue, we propose a dynamic semantic aware correlation modeling tracking framework. The core of our framework is a Dynamic Semantic Relevance Generator, which, in combination with the correlation map from the Transformer, explore semantic relevance. The approach enhances the search region's ability to extract important information from the template, improving accuracy and robustness under the aforementioned challenges. Additionally, to enhance the tracking speed, we design a pruning method for the proposed framework. Therefore, we present multiple model variants that achieve trade-offs between speed and accuracy, enabling flexible deployment according to the available computational resources. Experimental results validate the effectiveness of our method, achieving competitive performance on multiple UAV tracking datasets. The code is available at https://github.com/zxyyxzz/DSATrack."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21351","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:40Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:52:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21346","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21346","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21346","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lemin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lemin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangchao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Fangchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honghua","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Honghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaru","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yaru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Limin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Limin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongliang","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Yongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CT-CLIP: A Multi-modal Fusion Framework for Robust Apple Leaf Disease Recognition in Complex Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:23:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:43:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In complex orchard environments, the phenotypic heterogeneity of different apple leaf diseases, characterized by significant variation among lesions, poses a challenge to traditional multi-scale feature fusion methods. These methods only integrate multi-layer features extracted by convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and fail to adequately account for the relationships between local and global features. Therefore, this study proposes a multi-branch recognition framework named CNN-Transformer-CLIP (CT-CLIP). The framework synergistically employs a CNN to extract local lesion detail features and a Vision Transformer to capture global structural relationships. An Adaptive Feature Fusion Module (AFFM) then dynamically fuses these features, achieving optimal coupling of local and global information and effectively addressing the diversity in lesion morphology and distribution. Additionally, to mitigate interference from complex backgrounds and significantly enhance recognition accuracy under few-shot conditions, this study proposes a multimodal image-text learning approach. By leveraging pre-trained CLIP weights, it achieves deep alignment between visual features and disease semantic descriptions. Experimental results show that CT-CLIP achieves accuracies of 97.38% and 96.12% on a publicly available apple disease and a self-built dataset, outperforming several baseline methods. The proposed CT-CLIP demonstrates strong capabilities in recognizing agricultural diseases, significantly enhances identification accuracy under complex environmental conditions, provides an innovative and practical solution for automated disease recognition in agricultural applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21346","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:33Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:52:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21345","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21345","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21345","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aymane El","familyName":"Firdoussi","name":"Firdoussi, Aymane El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"El Mahdi","familyName":"Chayti","name":"Chayti, El Mahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed El Amine","familyName":"Seddik","name":"Seddik, Mohamed El Amine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Jaggi","name":"Jaggi, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"$α$-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:19:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:43:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-tuning has proven to be highly effective in adapting pre-trained models to perform better on new desired tasks with minimal data samples. Among the most widely used approaches are reparameterization methods, which update a target module by augmenting its frozen weight matrix with an additional trainable weight matrix. The most prominent example is Low Rank Adaption (LoRA), which gained significant attention in recent years. In this paper, we introduce a new class of reparameterization methods for transfer learning, designed to enhance the generalization ability of fine-tuned models. We establish the effectiveness of our approach in a high-dimensional binary classification setting using tools from Random Matrix Theory, and further validate our theoretical findings through more realistic experiments, such as fine-tuning LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21345","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:32Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:52:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21333","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21333","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21333","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunbo","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Yunbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianle","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Tianle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruijie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ruijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"He","name":"He, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiping","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guojie","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Guojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CausalRec: A CausalBoost Attention Model for Sequential Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:49:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:41:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in correlation-based sequential recommendation systems have demonstrated substantial success. Specifically, the attention-based model outperforms other RNN-based and Markov chains-based models by capturing both short- and long-term dependencies more effectively. However, solely focusing on item co-occurrences overlooks the underlying motivations behind user behaviors, leading to spurious correlations and potentially inaccurate recommendations. To address this limitation, we present a novel framework that integrates causal attention for sequential recommendation, CausalRec. It incorporates a causal discovery block and a CausalBooster. The causal discovery block learns the causal graph in user behavior sequences, and we provide a theory to guarantee the identifiability of the learned causal graph. The CausalBooster utilizes the discovered causal graph to refine the attention mechanism, prioritizing behaviors with causal significance. Experimental evaluations on real-world datasets indicate that CausalRec outperforms several state-of-the-art methods, with average improvements of 7.21% in Hit Rate (HR) and 8.65% in Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain (NDCG). To the best of our knowledge, this is the first model to incorporate causality through the attention mechanism in sequential recommendation, demonstrating the value of causality in generating more accurate and reliable recommendations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21333","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:16Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:52:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21282","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21282","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21282","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pavankumar","familyName":"Chandankar","name":"Chandankar, Pavankumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Burchard","name":"Burchard, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sensor-Specific Transformer (PatchTST) Ensembles with Test-Matched Augmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T09:26:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:37:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3714394.3756196","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a noise-aware, sensor-specific ensemble approach for robust human activity recognition on the 2nd WEAR Dataset Challenge. Our method leverages the PatchTST transformer architecture, training four independent models-one per inertial sensor location-on a tampered training set whose 1-second sliding windows are augmented to mimic the test-time noise. By aligning the train and test data schemas (JSON-encoded 50-sample windows) and applying randomized jitter, scaling, rotation, and channel dropout, each PatchTST model learns to generalize across real-world sensor perturbations. At inference, we compute softmax probabilities from all four sensor models on the Kaggle test set and average them to produce final labels. On the private leaderboard, this pipeline achieves a macro-F1 substantially above the baseline, demonstrating that test-matched augmentation combined with transformer-based ensembling is an effective strategy for robust HAR under noisy conditions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21282","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:03Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:51:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21267","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21267","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21267","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junshu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Junshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanxing","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Wanxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxue","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chenxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingming","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Qingming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Relieving the Over-Aggregating Effect in Graph Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:56:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:36:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graph attention has demonstrated superior performance in graph learning tasks. However, learning from global interactions can be challenging due to the large number of nodes. In this paper, we discover a new phenomenon termed over-aggregating. Over-aggregating arises when a large volume of messages is aggregated into a single node with less discrimination, leading to the dilution of the key messages and potential information loss. To address this, we propose Wideformer, a plug-and-play method for graph attention. Wideformer divides the aggregation of all nodes into parallel processes and guides the model to focus on specific subsets of these processes. The division can limit the input volume per aggregation, avoiding message dilution and reducing information loss. The guiding step sorts and weights the aggregation outputs, prioritizing the informative messages. Evaluations show that Wideformer can effectively mitigate over-aggregating. As a result, the backbone methods can focus on the informative messages, achieving superior performance compared to baseline methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21267","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:50:42Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:50:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:50:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21262","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21262","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21262","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Bonfanti","name":"Bonfanti, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ismael","familyName":"Medina","name":"Medina, Ismael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roman","familyName":"List","name":"List, Roman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Björn","familyName":"Staeves","name":"Staeves, Björn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto","familyName":"Santana","name":"Santana, Roberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Ellero","name":"Ellero, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PINN Balls: Scaling Second-Order Methods for PINNs with Domain Decomposition and Adaptive Sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:48:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:35:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Scientific Machine Learning have shown that second-order methods can enhance the training of Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs), making them a suitable alternative to traditional numerical methods for Partial Differential Equations (PDEs). However, second-order methods induce large memory requirements, making them scale poorly with the model size. In this paper, we define a local Mixture of Experts (MoE) combining the parameter-efficiency of ensemble models and sparse coding to enable the use of second-order training. Our model -- \\textsc{PINN Balls} -- also features a fully learnable domain decomposition structure, achieved through the use of Adversarial Adaptive Sampling (AAS), which adapts the DD to the PDE and its domain. \\textsc{PINN Balls} achieves better accuracy than the state-of-the-art in scientific machine learning, while maintaining invaluable scalability properties and drawing from a sound theoretical background."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted Conference Paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21262","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:50:35Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:50:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:50:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21258","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21258","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21258","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kumiko","familyName":"Tanaka-Ishii","name":"Tanaka-Ishii, Kumiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Correlation Dimension of Auto-Regressive Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Chaotic Dynamics (nlin.CD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:42:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:35:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable progress in natural language generation, yet they continue to display puzzling behaviors -- such as repetition and incoherence -- even when exhibiting low perplexity. This highlights a key limitation of conventional evaluation metrics, which emphasize local prediction accuracy while overlooking long-range structural complexity. We introduce correlation dimension, a fractal-geometric measure of self-similarity, to quantify the epistemological complexity of text as perceived by a language model. This measure captures the hierarchical recurrence structure of language, bridging local and global properties in a unified framework. Through extensive experiments, we show that correlation dimension (1) reveals three distinct phases during pretraining, (2) reflects context-dependent complexity, (3) indicates a model's tendency toward hallucination, and (4) reliably detects multiple forms of degeneration in generated text. The method is computationally efficient, robust to model quantization (down to 4-bit precision), broadly applicable across autoregressive architectures (e.g., Transformer and Mamba), and provides fresh insight into the generative dynamics of LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21258","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:50:29Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:50:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:50:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21254","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21254","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21254","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victoria J.","familyName":"Hodge","name":"Hodge, Victoria J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Colin","familyName":"Paterson","name":"Paterson, Colin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim","familyName":"Habli","name":"Habli, Ibrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Out-of-Distribution Detection for Safety Assurance of AI and Autonomous Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:38:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:34:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The operational capabilities and application domains of AI-enabled autonomous systems have expanded significantly in recent years due to advances in robotics and machine learning (ML). Demonstrating the safety of autonomous systems rigorously is critical for their responsible adoption but it is challenging as it requires robust methodologies that can handle novel and uncertain situations throughout the system lifecycle, including detecting out-of-distribution (OoD) data. Thus, OOD detection is receiving increased attention from the research, development and safety engineering communities. This comprehensive review analyses OOD detection techniques within the context of safety assurance for autonomous systems, in particular in safety-critical domains. We begin by defining the relevant concepts, investigating what causes OOD and exploring the factors which make the safety assurance of autonomous systems and OOD detection challenging. Our review identifies a range of techniques which can be used throughout the ML development lifecycle and we suggest areas within the lifecycle in which they may be used to support safety assurance arguments. We discuss a number of caveats that system and safety engineers must be aware of when integrating OOD detection into system lifecycles. We conclude by outlining the challenges and future work necessary for the safe development and operation of autonomous systems across a range of domains and applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21254","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:50:24Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:50:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:50:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21209","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21209","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21209","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixiang","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Zixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guochang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Guochang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifeng","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianqiang","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Jianqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpecTokenizer: A Lightweight Streaming Codec in the Compressed Spectrum Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T07:25:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:30:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.21437/interspeech.2025-546","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural Audio Codecs (NACs) have gained growing attention in recent years as technologies for audio compression and audio representation in speech language models. While mainstream NACs typically require G-level computation and M-level parameters, the performance of lightweight and streaming NACs remains underexplored. This paper proposes SpecTokenizer, a lightweight streaming codec that operates in the compressed spectral domain. Composed solely of alternating CNN and RNN layers, SpecTokenizer achieves greater efficiency and better representational capability through multi-scale modeling in the compressed spectrum domain. At 4 kbps, the proposed SpecTokenizer achieves comparable or superior performance compared to the codec with state-of-the-art lightweight architecture while requiring only 20% of the computation and 10% of the parameters. Furthermore, it significantly outperforms the codec when using similar computational and storage resources."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by Interspeech 2025; 5 pages, 1 figure, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21209","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:19Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:49:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21207","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21207","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21207","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunlong","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Yunlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minglai","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Minglai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zengyi","familyName":"Wo","name":"Wo, Zengyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruijie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ruijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jianxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Graph Mixture of Residual Experts: Unsupervised Learning on Diverse Graphs with Heterogeneous Specialization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T07:18:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:30:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) face a fundamental adaptability challenge: their fixed message-passing architectures struggle with the immense diversity of real-world graphs, where optimal computational strategies vary by local structure and task. While Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) offers a promising pathway to adaptability, existing graph MoE methods remain constrained by their reliance on supervised signals and instability when training heterogeneous experts. We introduce ADaMoRE (Adaptive Mixture of Residual Experts), a principled framework that enables robust, fully unsupervised training of heterogeneous MoE on graphs. ADaMoRE employs a backbone-residual expert architecture where foundational encoders provide stability while specialized residual experts capture diverse computational patterns. A structurally-aware gating network performs fine-grained node routing. The entire architecture is trained end-to-end using a unified unsupervised objective, which integrates a primary reconstruction task with an information-theoretic diversity regularizer to explicitly enforce functional specialization among the experts. Theoretical analysis confirms our design improves data efficiency and training stability. Extensive evaluation across 16 benchmarks validates ADaMoRE's state-of-the-art performance in unsupervised node classification and few-shot learning, alongside superior generalization, training efficiency, and faster convergence on diverse graphs and tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21207","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:16Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:49:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21181","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21181","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21181","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keqin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Keqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shylock: Causal Discovery in Multivariate Time Series based on Hybrid Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T06:12:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:27:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Causal relationship discovery has been drawing increasing attention due to its prevalent application. Existing methods rely on human experience, statistical methods, or graphical criteria methods which are error-prone, stuck at the idealized assumption, and rely on a huge amount of data. And there is also a serious data gap in accessing Multivariate time series(MTS) in many areas, adding difficulty in finding their causal relationship. Existing methods are easy to be over-fitting on them. To fill the gap we mentioned above, in this paper, we propose Shylock, a novel method that can work well in both few-shot and normal MTS to find the causal relationship. Shylock can reduce the number of parameters exponentially by using group dilated convolution and a sharing kernel, but still learn a better representation of variables with time delay. By combing the global constraint and the local constraint, Shylock achieves information sharing among networks to help improve the accuracy. To evaluate the performance of Shylock, we also design a data generation method to generate MTS with time delay. We evaluate it on commonly used benchmarks and generated datasets. Extensive experiments show that Shylock outperforms two existing state-of-art methods on both few-shot and normal MTS. We also developed Tcausal, a library for easy use and deployed it on the EarthDataMiner platform"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21181","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:39Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:48:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21168","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21168","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21168","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandra","familyName":"Ranilla-Cortina","name":"Ranilla-Cortina, Sandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diego A.","familyName":"Aranda","name":"Aranda, Diego A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jorge","familyName":"Ballesteros","name":"Ballesteros, Jorge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesus","familyName":"Bonilla","name":"Bonilla, Jesus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nerea","familyName":"Monrio","name":"Monrio, Nerea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elías F.","familyName":"Combarro","name":"Combarro, Elías F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose","familyName":"Ranilla","name":"Ranilla, Jose","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum Neural Network Architectures for Multivariate Time-Series Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T05:44:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:26:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we address the challenge of multivariate time-series forecasting using quantum machine learning techniques. We introduce adaptation strategies that extend variational quantum circuit models, traditionally limited to univariate data, toward the multivariate setting, exploring both purely quantum and hybrid quantum-classical formulations. First, we extend and benchmark several VQC-based and hybrid architectures to systematically evaluate their capacity to model cross-variable dependencies. Second, building upon these foundations, we introduce the iQTransformer, a novel quantum transformer architecture that integrates a quantum self-attention mechanism within the iTransformer framework, enabling a quantum-native representation of inter-variable relationships. Third, we provide a comprehensive empirical evaluation on both synthetic and real-world datasets, showing that quantum-based models may achieve competitive or superior forecasting accuracy with fewer trainable parameters and faster convergence than state-of-the-art classical and quantum baselines in some cases. These contributions highlight the potential of quantum-enhanced architectures as efficient and scalable tools for advancing multivariate time-series forecasting."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21168","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:20Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:48:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21160","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21160","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21160","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanlin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Guanlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyan","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Boyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichen","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao Frank","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hao Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Physics-informed Spatial Intelligence with Human Priors: An Autonomous Driving Pilot Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T05:21:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:25:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"How to integrate and verify spatial intelligence in foundation models remains an open challenge. Current practice often proxies Visual-Spatial Intelligence (VSI) with purely textual prompts and VQA-style scoring, which obscures geometry, invites linguistic shortcuts, and weakens attribution to genuinely spatial skills. We introduce Spatial Intelligence Grid (SIG): a structured, grid-based schema that explicitly encodes object layouts, inter-object relations, and physically grounded priors. As a complementary channel to text, SIG provides a faithful, compositional representation of scene structure for foundation-model reasoning. Building on SIG, we derive SIG-informed evaluation metrics that quantify a model's intrinsic VSI, which separates spatial capability from language priors. In few-shot in-context learning with state-of-the-art multimodal LLMs (e.g. GPT- and Gemini-family models), SIG yields consistently larger, more stable, and more comprehensive gains across all VSI metrics compared to VQA-only representations, indicating its promise as a data-labeling and training schema for learning VSI. We also release SIGBench, a benchmark of 1.4K driving frames annotated with ground-truth SIG labels and human gaze traces, supporting both grid-based machine VSI tasks and attention-driven, human-like VSI tasks in autonomous-driving scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 (Spotlight)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21160","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:09Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:48:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21142","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21142","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21142","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hossein","familyName":"Adeli","name":"Adeli, Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolaus","familyName":"Kriegeskorte","name":"Kriegeskorte, Nikolaus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"In Silico Mapping of Visual Categorical Selectivity Across the Whole Brain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T04:26:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:23:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A fine-grained account of functional selectivity in the cortex is essential for understanding how visual information is processed and represented in the brain. Classical studies using designed experiments have identified multiple category-selective regions; however, these approaches rely on preconceived hypotheses about categories. Subsequent data-driven discovery methods have sought to address this limitation but are often limited by simple, typically linear encoding models. We propose an in silico approach for data-driven discovery of novel category-selectivity hypotheses based on an encoder-decoder transformer model. The architecture incorporates a brain-region to image-feature cross-attention mechanism, enabling nonlinear mappings between high-dimensional deep network features and semantic patterns encoded in the brain activity. We further introduce a method to characterize the selectivity of individual parcels by leveraging diffusion-based image generative models and large-scale datasets to synthesize and select images that maximally activate each parcel. Our approach reveals regions with complex, compositional selectivity involving diverse semantic concepts, which we validate in silico both within and across subjects. Using a brain encoder as a \"digital twin\" offers a powerful, data-driven framework for generating and testing hypotheses about visual selectivity in the human brain - hypotheses that can guide future fMRI experiments. Our code is available at: https://kriegeskorte-lab.github.io/in-silico-mapping/ ."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21142","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:44Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21114","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21114","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21114","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanguang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yanguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Controllable-LPMoE: Adapting to Challenging Object Segmentation via Dynamic Local Priors from Mixture-of-Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:03:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:20:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale foundation models provide powerful feature representations for downstream object segmentation tasks. However, when adapted to specific tasks through the full-parameter fine-tuning, the enormous parameters being updated often results in significant computational overhead, creating a bottleneck in training efficiency. Although existing methods attempt to fine-tune frozen models by directly embedding trainable prompts, these prompts lack inherent semantic priors, limiting the adaptability of large-scale models. In this paper, we propose a novel dynamic priors-based fine-tuning paradigm with fewer trainable parameters, dubbed Controllable-LPMoE, which adaptively modulates frozen foundation models by dynamically controlling local priors to enhance fine-grained perception for specific segmentation tasks. More specifically, we construct a lightweight dynamic mixed local priors extractor that captures diverse local priors from input images through heterogeneous convolutions while employing a gating network to dynamically output expert priors required for the subsequent fine-tuning. Furthermore, we design a bi-directional interaction adapter that employs cosine-aligned deformable attention and channel-oriented adaptive scale enhancement to interact and restructure between frozen and trainable features, achieving efficient fine-tuning. Extensive experiments validate the superiority of our \\href{https://github.com/CSYSI/Controllable-LPMoE} {Controllable-LPMoE} approach, demonstrating excellent segmentation performance compared to 31 state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods and adaptability to multiple binary object segmentation tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21114","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:04Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:47:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21093","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21093","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21093","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Siyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinbo","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Jinbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tenghui","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Tenghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xumin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanjia","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yuanjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hudan","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Hudan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishao","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Zishao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dusit","familyName":"Niyato","name":"Niyato, Dusit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengli","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Shengli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong In","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dong In","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MedAlign: A Synergistic Framework of Multimodal Preference Optimization and Federated Meta-Cognitive Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T02:11:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:17:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, large models have shown significant potential for smart healthcare. However, the deployment of Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) for clinical services is currently hindered by three critical challenges: a tendency to hallucinate answers not grounded in visual evidence, the inefficiency of fixed-depth reasoning, and the difficulty of multi-institutional collaboration. To address these challenges, in this paper, we develop MedAlign, a novel framework to ensure visually accurate LVLM responses for Medical Visual Question Answering (Med-VQA). Specifically, we first propose a multimodal Direct Preference Optimization (mDPO) objective to explicitly align preference learning with visual context. We then design a Retrieval-Aware Mixture-of-Experts (RA-MoE) architecture that utilizes image and text similarity to route queries to a specialized and context-augmented LVLM (i.e., an expert), thereby mitigating hallucinations in LVLMs. To achieve adaptive reasoning and facilitate multi-institutional collaboration, we propose a federated governance mechanism, where the selected expert, fine-tuned on clinical datasets based on mDPO, locally performs iterative Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning via the local meta-cognitive uncertainty estimator. Extensive experiments on three representative Med-VQA datasets demonstrate that MedAlign achieves state-of-the-art performance, outperforming strong retrieval-augmented baselines by up to $11.85\\%$ in F1-score, and simultaneously reducing the average reasoning length by $51.60\\%$ compared with fixed-depth CoT approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21093","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:35Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21088","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21088","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21088","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyang","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Hongyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"M-GLC: Motif-Driven Global-Local Context Graphs for Few-shot Molecular Property Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T02:00:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:16:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Molecular property prediction (MPP) is a cornerstone of drug discovery and materials science, yet conventional deep learning approaches depend on large labeled datasets that are often unavailable. Few-shot Molecular property prediction (FSMPP) addresses this scarcity by incorporating relational inductive bias through a context graph that links molecule nodes to property nodes, but such molecule-property graphs offer limited structural guidance. We propose a comprehensive solution: Motif Driven Global-Local Context Graph for few-shot molecular property prediction, which enriches contextual information at both the global and local levels. At the global level, chemically meaningful motif nodes representing shared substructures, such as rings or functional groups, are introduced to form a global tri-partite heterogeneous graph, yielding motif-molecule-property connections that capture long-range compositional patterns and enable knowledge transfer among molecules with common motifs. At the local level, we build a subgraph for each node in the molecule-property pair and encode them separately to concentrate the model's attention on the most informative neighboring molecules and motifs. Experiments on five standard FSMPP benchmarks demonstrate that our framework consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods. These results underscore the effectiveness of integrating global motif knowledge with fine-grained local context to advance robust few-shot molecular property prediction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21088","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:28Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21079","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21079","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21079","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Guoan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjing","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Wenjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangwei","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Guangwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guo-Jun","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Guo-Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia-Wen","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chia-Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WaveSeg: Enhancing Segmentation Precision via High-Frequency Prior and Mamba-Driven Spectrum Decomposition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T01:41:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:15:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While recent semantic segmentation networks heavily rely on powerful pretrained encoders, most employ simplistic decoders, leading to suboptimal trade-offs between semantic context and fine-grained detail preservation. To address this, we propose a novel decoder architecture, WaveSeg, which jointly optimizes feature refinement in spatial and wavelet domains. Specifically, high-frequency components are first learned from input images as explicit priors to reinforce boundary details at early stages. A multi-scale fusion mechanism, Dual Domain Operation (DDO), is then applied, and the novel Spectrum Decomposition Attention (SDA) block is proposed, which is developed to leverage Mamba's linear-complexity long-range modeling to enhance high-frequency structural details. Meanwhile, reparameterized convolutions are applied to preserve low-frequency semantic integrity in the wavelet domain. Finally, a residual-guided fusion integrates multi-scale features with boundary-aware representations at native resolution, producing semantically and structurally rich feature maps. Extensive experiments on standard benchmarks demonstrate that WaveSeg, leveraging wavelet-domain frequency prior with Mamba-based attention, consistently outperforms state-of-the-art approaches both quantitatively and qualitatively, achieving efficient and precise segmentation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21079","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:15Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:46:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21048","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21048","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21048","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiabo","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jiabo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimitrios","familyName":"Pezaros","name":"Pezaros, Dimitrios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yehia","familyName":"Elkhatib","name":"Elkhatib, Yehia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"xMem: A CPU-Based Approach for Accurate Estimation of GPU Memory in Deep Learning Training Workloads"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T23:16:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:12:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3721462.3770773","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The global scarcity of GPUs necessitates more sophisticated strategies for Deep Learning jobs in shared cluster environments. Accurate estimation of how much GPU memory a job will require is fundamental to enabling advanced scheduling and GPU sharing, which helps prevent out-of-memory (OOM) errors and resource underutilization. However, existing estimation methods have limitations. Approaches relying on static analysis or historical data with machine learning often fail to accurately capture runtime dynamics. Furthermore, direct GPU analysis consumes scarce resources, and some techniques require intrusive code modifications. Thus, the key challenge lies in precisely estimating dynamic memory requirements, including memory allocator nuances, without consuming GPU resources and non-intrusive code changes. To address this challenge, we propose xMem, a novel framework that leverages CPU-only dynamic analysis to accurately estimate peak GPU memory requirements a priori. We conducted a thorough evaluation of xMem against state-of-the-art solutions using workloads from 25 different models, including architectures like Convolutional Neural Networks and Transformers. The analysis of 5209 runs, which includes ANOVA and Monte Carlo results, highlights xMem's benefits: it decreases the median relative error by 91% and significantly reduces the probability of estimation failure as safe OOM thresholds by 75%, meaning that the estimated value can often be used directly without causing OOM. Ultimately, these improvements lead to a 368% increase in memory conservation potential over current solutions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21048","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:31Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:45:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:45:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Mahdi Danesh","familyName":"Pajouh","name":"Pajouh, Mohammad Mahdi Danesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Saeedi","name":"Saeedi, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Meningioma Tumor Segmentation Using Ensemble Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T22:51:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:11:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Meningiomas represent the most prevalent form of primary brain tumors, comprising nearly one-third of all diagnosed cases. Accurate delineation of these tumors from MRI scans is crucial for guiding treatment strategies, yet remains a challenging and time-consuming task in clinical practice. Recent developments in deep learning have accelerated progress in automated tumor segmentation; however, many advanced techniques are hindered by heavy computational demands and long training schedules, making them less accessible for researchers and clinicians working with limited hardware. In this work, we propose a novel ensemble-based segmentation approach that combines three distinct architectures: (1) a baseline SegResNet model, (2) an attention-augmented SegResNet with concatenative skip connections, and (3) a dual-decoder U-Net enhanced with attention-gated skip connections (DDUNet). The ensemble aims to leverage architectural diversity to improve robustness and accuracy while significantly reducing training demands. Each baseline model was trained for only 20 epochs and Evaluated on the BraTS-MEN 2025 dataset. The proposed ensemble model achieved competitive performance, with average Lesion-Wise Dice scores of 77.30%, 76.37% and 73.9% on test dataset for Enhancing Tumor (ET), Tumor Core (TC) and Whole Tumor (WT) respectively. These results highlight the effectiveness of ensemble learning for brain tumor segmentation, even under limited hardware constraints. Our proposed method provides a practical and accessible tool for aiding the diagnosis of meningioma, with potential impact in both clinical and research settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"2nd Place Winner in the BraTS 2025 MICCAI Challenge (Task 2: Meningioma Tumor Segmentation)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:20Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:45:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:45:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21021","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21021","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21021","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxin","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Xiaoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengkai","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Chengkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongtao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Hongtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lina","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Lina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gaussian Mixture Flow Matching with Domain Alignment for Multi-Domain Sequential Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T22:11:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:10:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Users increasingly interact with content across multiple domains, resulting in sequential behaviors marked by frequent and complex transitions. While Cross-Domain Sequential Recommendation (CDSR) models two-domain interactions, Multi-Domain Sequential Recommendation (MDSR) introduces significantly more domain transitions, compounded by challenges such as domain heterogeneity and imbalance. Existing approaches often overlook the intricacies of domain transitions, tend to overfit to dense domains while underfitting sparse ones, and struggle to scale effectively as the number of domains increases. We propose \\textit{GMFlowRec}, an efficient generative framework for MDSR that models domain-aware transition trajectories via Gaussian Mixture Flow Matching. GMFlowRec integrates: (1) a unified dual-masked Transformer to disentangle domain-invariant and domain-specific intents, (2) a Gaussian Mixture flow field to capture diverse behavioral patterns, and (3) a domain-aligned prior to support frequent and sparse transitions. Extensive experiments on JD and Amazon datasets demonstrate that GMFlowRec achieves state-of-the-art performance with up to 44\\% improvement in NDCG@5, while maintaining high efficiency via a single unified backbone, making it scalable for real-world multi-domain sequential recommendation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21021","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:44:54Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:44:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:44:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20985","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20985","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20985","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizhao","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zhizhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoxin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Puyang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Puyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GPU Memory Requirement Prediction for Deep Learning Task Based on Bidirectional Gated Recurrent Unit Optimization Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T20:20:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:07:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In response to the increasingly critical demand for accurate prediction of GPU memory resources in deep learning tasks, this paper deeply analyzes the current research status and innovatively proposes a deep learning model that integrates bidirectional gated recurrent units (BiGRU) to optimize the Transformer architecture, aiming to improve the accuracy of memory demand prediction. To verify the effectiveness of the model, a carefully designed comparative experiment was conducted, selecting four representative basic machine learning models: decision tree, random forest, Adaboost, and XGBoost as benchmarks. The detailed experimental results show that the BiGRU Transformer optimization model proposed in this paper exhibits significant advantages in key evaluation indicators: in terms of mean square error (MSE) and root mean square error (RMSE), the model achieves the lowest value among all comparison models, and its predicted results have the smallest deviation from the actual values; In terms of mean absolute error (MAE) and coefficient of determination (R2) indicators, the model also performs well and the results are balanced and stable, with comprehensive predictive performance far exceeding the benchmark machine learning methods compared. In summary, the Transformer model based on bidirectional gated recurrent unit optimization successfully constructed in this study can efficiently and accurately complete GPU memory demand prediction tasks in deep learning tasks, and its prediction accuracy has been significantly improved compared to traditional machine learning methods. This research provides strong technical support and reliable theoretical basis for optimizing resource scheduling and management of deep learning tasks, and improving the utilization efficiency of computing clusters."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20985","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:44:04Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:44:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:44:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20952","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20952","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20952","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungjun","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Sungjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changho","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Changho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suenggwan","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Suenggwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinya","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xinya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shourjo Aditya","familyName":"Chaudhuri","name":"Chaudhuri, Shourjo Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frederic","familyName":"Sala","name":"Sala, Frederic","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Models for Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T19:28:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:05:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Forecasting in the real world requires integrating structured time-series data with unstructured textual information, but existing methods are architecturally limited by fixed input/output horizons and are unable to model or quantify uncertainty. We address this challenge by introducing LLM-integrated Bayesian State space models (LBS), a novel probabilistic framework for multimodal temporal forecasting. At a high level, LBS consists of two components: (1) a state space model (SSM) backbone that captures the temporal dynamics of latent states from which both numerical and textual observations are generated and (2) a pretrained large language model (LLM) that is adapted to encode textual inputs for posterior state estimation and decode textual forecasts consistent with the latent trajectory. This design enables flexible lookback and forecast windows, principled uncertainty quantification, and improved temporal generalization thanks to the well-suited inductive bias of SSMs toward modeling dynamical systems. Experiments on the TextTimeCorpus benchmark demonstrate that LBS improves the previous state-of-the-art by 13.20% while providing human-readable summaries of each forecast. Our work is the first to unify LLMs and SSMs for joint numerical and textual prediction, offering a novel foundation for multimodal temporal reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20952","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:18Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:43:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20943","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20943","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20943","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srivathsan","familyName":"Badrinarayanan","name":"Badrinarayanan, Srivathsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Janghoon","familyName":"Ock","name":"Ock, Janghoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Alan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanya","familyName":"Ahuja","name":"Ahuja, Sanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir Barati","familyName":"Farimani","name":"Farimani, Amir Barati","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Meta-Learning for Cross-Task Generalization in Protein Mutation Property Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T19:09:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:04:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Protein mutations can have profound effects on biological function, making accurate prediction of property changes critical for drug discovery, protein engineering, and precision medicine. Current approaches rely on fine-tuning protein-specific transformers for individual datasets, but struggle with cross-dataset generalization due to heterogeneous experimental conditions and limited target domain data. We introduce two key innovations: (1) the first application of Model-Agnostic Meta-Learning (MAML) to protein mutation property prediction, and (2) a novel mutation encoding strategy using separator tokens to directly incorporate mutations into sequence context. We build upon transformer architectures integrating them with MAML to enable rapid adaptation to new tasks through minimal gradient steps rather than learning dataset-specific patterns. Our mutation encoding addresses the critical limitation where standard transformers treat mutation positions as unknown tokens, significantly degrading performance. Evaluation across three diverse protein mutation datasets (functional fitness, thermal stability, and solubility) demonstrates significant advantages over traditional fine-tuning. In cross-task evaluation, our meta-learning approach achieves 29% better accuracy for functional fitness with 65% less training time, and 94% better accuracy for solubility with 55% faster training. The framework maintains consistent training efficiency regardless of dataset size, making it particularly valuable for industrial applications and early-stage protein design where experimental data is limited. This work establishes a systematic application of meta-learning to protein mutation analysis and introduces an effective mutation encoding strategy, offering transformative methodology for cross-domain generalization in protein engineering."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20943","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:05Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:43:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20888","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20888","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20888","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zenan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zenan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiancheng","familyName":"Zhi","name":"Zhi, Tiancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shen","familyName":"Sang","name":"Sang, Shen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linjie","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Linjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Video-As-Prompt: Unified Semantic Control for Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T17:59:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:02:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unified, generalizable semantic control in video generation remains a critical open challenge. Existing methods either introduce artifacts by enforcing inappropriate pixel-wise priors from structure-based controls, or rely on non-generalizable, condition-specific finetuning or task-specific architectures. We introduce Video-As-Prompt (VAP), a new paradigm that reframes this problem as in-context generation. VAP leverages a reference video as a direct semantic prompt, guiding a frozen Video Diffusion Transformer (DiT) via a plug-and-play Mixture-of-Transformers (MoT) expert. This architecture prevents catastrophic forgetting and is guided by a temporally biased position embedding that eliminates spurious mapping priors for robust context retrieval. To power this approach and catalyze future research, we built VAP-Data, the largest dataset for semantic-controlled video generation with over 100K paired videos across 100 semantic conditions. As a single unified model, VAP sets a new state-of-the-art for open-source methods, achieving a 38.7% user preference rate that rivals leading condition-specific commercial models. VAP's strong zero-shot generalization and support for various downstream applications mark a significant advance toward general-purpose, controllable video generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Website: https://bytedance.github.io/Video-As-Prompt"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20888","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:47Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20868","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20868","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20868","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Crisis-Resilient Portfolio Management via Graph-based Spatio-Temporal Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T06:23:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:01:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Financial time series forecasting faces a fundamental challenge: predicting optimal asset allocations requires understanding regime-dependent correlation structures that transform during crisis periods. Existing graph-based spatio-temporal learning approaches rely on predetermined graph topologies--correlation thresholds, sector classifications--that fail to adapt when market dynamics shift across different crisis mechanisms: credit contagion, pandemic shocks, or inflation-driven selloffs.\n We present CRISP (Crisis-Resilient Investment through Spatio-temporal Patterns), a graph-based spatio-temporal learning framework that encodes spatial relationships via Graph Convolutional Networks and temporal dynamics via BiLSTM with self-attention, then learns sparse structures through multi-head Graph Attention Networks. Unlike fixed-topology methods, CRISP discovers which asset relationships matter through attention mechanisms, filtering 92.5% of connections as noise while preserving crisis-relevant dependencies for accurate regime-specific predictions.\n Trained on 2005--2021 data encompassing credit and pandemic crises, CRISP demonstrates robust generalization to 2022--2024 inflation-driven markets--a fundamentally different regime--by accurately forecasting regime-appropriate correlation structures. This enables adaptive portfolio allocation that maintains profitability during downturns, achieving Sharpe ratio 3.76: 707% improvement over equal-weight baselines and 94% improvement over static graph methods. Learned attention weights provide interpretable regime detection, with defensive cluster attention strengthening 49% during crises versus 31% market-wide--emergent behavior from learning to forecast rather than imposing assumptions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20868","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:18Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:41:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20829","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20829","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20829","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Fa","name":"Fa, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milan","familyName":"Buttberg","name":"Buttberg, Milan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Ran","name":"Ran, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rana Walied","familyName":"Ahmad","name":"Ahmad, Rana Walied","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dennis","familyName":"Braun","name":"Braun, Dennis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Völkel","name":"Völkel, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jimin","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofía","familyName":"Cruces","name":"Cruces, Sofía","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bart","familyName":"Macco","name":"Macco, Bart","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bárbara","familyName":"Canto","name":"Canto, Bárbara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Holger","familyName":"Lerch","name":"Lerch, Holger","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thorsten","familyName":"Wahlbrink","name":"Wahlbrink, Thorsten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Holger","familyName":"Kalisch","name":"Kalisch, Holger","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Heuken","name":"Heuken, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrei","familyName":"Vescan","name":"Vescan, Andrei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joachim","familyName":"Mayer","name":"Mayer, Joachim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenxing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilia","familyName":"Valov","name":"Valov, Ilia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephan","familyName":"Menzel","name":"Menzel, Stephan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max C.","familyName":"Lemme","name":"Lemme, Max C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Intermediate Resistive State in Wafer-Scale MoS${_2}$ Memristors through Lateral Silver Filament Growth for Artificial Synapse Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Applied Physics (physics.app-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-09T19:20:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:00:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Memristors based on two-dimensional materials (2DMs) have garnered significant attention due to their fast resistive switching (RS) behavior and atomic-level thickness, which enables low power consumption, making them promising candidates for neuromorphic computing. Among these, memristors based on molybdenum disulfide (MoS${_2}$) have been extensively studied. Their RS has been attributed to the formation and rupture of conductive filaments (CFs). However, the underlying mechanism of filament formation remains underexplored, and the inherently stochastic nature of RS leads to high variability and limited reproducibility. Additionally, the lack of scalable fabrication techniques for 2DM-based memristors restricts their integration into standard semiconductor technology. Here, we demonstrate memristors based on metal-organic chemical vapor-deposited MoS${_2}$ on the wafer-scale. Our devices exhibit volatile and nonvolatile RS behavior, tunable by modulating the current compliance. Notably, we observe stable RS characteristics in an intermediate resistive state (IRS), featuring set and reset voltages within $\\pm$1 V, an endurance exceeding 2500 cycles in direct current mode, and a state retention over 10${^6}$ s. The experimental data, complemented with simulations, suggest that the IRS originates from the lateral growth of the CF within the MoS${_2}$ layer. Furthermore, the devices successfully emulate synaptic plasticity with current responses on the microsecond timescale, highlighting their potential for large-scale integration in neuromorphic computing architectures."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20829","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:40:23Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:40:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:40:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":64,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-26T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-27T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+%22mixture-of-experts%22+OR+attention+OR+%22state+space+model%22+OR+%22state-space+models%22%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+%22mixture-of-experts%22+OR+attention+OR+%22state+space+model%22+OR+%22state-space+models%22%29%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100"}}